<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sullivan Expedition</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1779">1779</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Sullivan-Clinton Expedition, Sullivan Campaign, Sullivan-Clinton Campaign</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sullivan Expedition was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> military campaign of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a>, from June to October 1779, against the four British-allied nations of the Haudenosaunee (Iroquois) Confederacy in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. Ordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a> after Haudenosaunee and Loyalist raids on the Wyoming Valley and Cherry Valley, four Continental Army <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">brigades</a> under John Sullivan and James Clinton carried out a scorched-earth campaign, razing 40 villages with their crops and food stores. Just over 5,000 Haudenosaunee fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-niagara">Fort Niagara</a>, where many starved or froze to death that winter. Some scholars describe the campaign as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>, a term that is disputed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sullivan-expedition.jpg" alt="Sullivan Expedition" width="330" height="186" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sullivan01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>When the Revolutionary War began, both the British and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/continental-congress">Continental Congress</a> sought the allegiance of the Haudenosaunee, or Six Nations. Most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-people">Senecas</a>, Cayugas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onondaga-people">Onondagas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawks</a> sided with the British, while most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oneida-people">Oneidas</a> and Tuscaroras joined the revolutionaries, partly through the influence of the Presbyterian missionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-kirkland">Samuel Kirkland</a>. After the surrender of General John Burgoyne at Saratoga in October 1777, raids from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-niagara">Fort Niagara</a> led by the Loyalist Major John Butler, the Mohawk leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-brant">Joseph Brant</a> and the Seneca war chiefs Sayenqueraghta and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornplanter">Cornplanter</a> struck <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-frontier">American frontier</a> settlements and Oneida villages. A raid on Cobleskill on 30 May 1778 killed 22 regulars and militia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The expedition took up more than a third of the Continental Army: four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">brigades</a>, 4,469 men on paper and just under 4,000 when it set out. Sullivan marched from Easton, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, on 18 June, reached the Wyoming Valley on 23 June and, delayed by supply shortages, moved up the Susquehanna to Tioga, where Fort Sullivan was built. James Clinton's brigade dammed Otsego Lake to float its boats downriver and joined him on 22 August.</p><p>On 29 August Sullivan's army of 3,200 met about 600 Loyalists and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-people">Seneca</a>, Cayuga and Delaware warriors behind a log breastwork at Newtown on the Chemung River. A flanking march through a morass came too late, and Butler withdrew. The army then burned Catherine's Town, Kanadaseaga and Chenussio, whose 128 houses were destroyed with their fields, and detachments laid waste the Cayuga villages around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cayuga-lake">Cayuga Lake</a>. Colonel Daniel Brodhead led a separate force up the Allegheny from Fort Pitt, and Colonel Peter Gansevoort seized the neutral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> settlement of Tiononderoga. The army abandoned Fort Sullivan on 3 October.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>On 21 September there were 5,036 refugees at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-niagara">Fort Niagara</a>, and about 2,600 by 21 November; most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-people">Seneca</a> and Cayuga who wintered there later settled at Buffalo Creek. The winter of 1780 was bitterly cold, and estimates of the dead vary widely: Jeffrey Ostler counts about 200 killed by military action and up to 1,000 later deaths, Colin Calloway and Alan Taylor several hundred, while Koehler suggests roughly 5,000. Sullivan, his health broken, resigned on 30 November 1779. Monuments marked its centenary in 1879 and its 150th anniversary in 1929, the 35 of 1929 with plaques claiming that Sullivan had forever stopped Indian depredations, a narrative now regarded as false.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sullivan Expedition” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1779 the <b>Sullivan Expedition</b> destroyed some forty Haudenosaunee towns and their crops, and the Treaty of Paris of 1783 made no provision for Britain’s Native allies.</blockquote><a href="/empire/iroquois-confederacy">Iroquois Confederacy · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>The Revolution broke the League apart, and after the <b>Sullivan Expedition</b> of 1779 destroyed 40 to 60 towns, more than 5,000 Haudenosaunee fled to British Canada.</blockquote><a href="/societies/haudenosaunee">Social Hierarchy of the Haudenosaunee</a></li></ul></section>
